# Debugging and tracing

```elixir
System.put_env("AL_MNESIA_DISTRIBUTED", "false")

System.put_env(
  "AL_MNESIA_DIR",
  Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "al_livebook_#{:erlang.unique_integer([:positive])}")
)

Mix.install(
  [{:al, github: "anoma/AL-Ex"}],
  config: [al: [transaction_programs: [:bootstrap], natives: []]]
)
```

## Setup

This notebook is self-contained. The cell above installs AL directly, no separate node, no cookie, nothing to attach to. The first run compiles AL and its dependencies from scratch, which takes a minute or two. Reopening this notebook later is fast, since `Mix.install` caches by the exact dependency and config combination. State here is throwaway. It lives only for this notebook's session and disappears when the session ends.

If you already have a real AL node running and want the persistent, shared store instead, see the [intro](./intro.livemd) for how to attach to it instead.

`Mix.install` has to finish before `use AL` loads the library, so that happens in its own cell here.

```elixir
use AL

AL.Branch.head()
```

## Reading a failure

An aborted `run` comes back with a structured reason naming exactly what went wrong. Sending a selector a method it does not understand names the receiver, the selector, its arity, and a ranked list of suggestions.

```elixir
result =
  run(~S"""
    @failgreeter
    #{super => value}.

    failgreeter >> init
    | Self _ Self |.

    failgreeter >> greet
    | Self _Name |.

    new failgreeter G.
    greett G world.
    """)

{:aborted, reason} = result
{reason.reason, reason.message}
```

AL's reporter can also report domain violations.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  @failure_domain_probe
  #{super => object, ivars => [#{domain => ["on", "off"], name => state}]}.

  new failure_domain_probe #{name => failure_domain_instance, state => "on"} _.
  """)
```

## A custom `does_not_understand` suppresses the failure entirely

If a class handles its own misses, sending it something unknown doesn't abort. This allows for custom fallback behaviour.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  @failquiet
  #{super => value}.

  failquiet >> init
  | Self _ Self |.

  failquiet >> does_not_understand
  | Self _M _A |.

  new failquiet Q.
  anything Q x.
  """)
```

## The domino tracing model

AL's call-tree trace family is called "Domino" and is built on the classic Prolog tracer model: a "Byrd box" for every call, with four ports: `Call`, `Exit`, `Redo`, `Fail`. Plain Prolog needs one box per predicate call. AL needs two, stacked: a **method** box (dispatch choosing which provider answers a selector), which wraps a **clause** box (which clause of the chosen method actually runs). Every send opens because AL has dispatch sitting on top of clause selection, whereas Prolog only has the latter.

Retained traces are opt-in and composable. Use `trace: [:domino]` when you need the structured evidence stream; ordinary runs retain no trace.

```elixir
{:atomic, {bindings, state}} =
  run(~S"""
    fibonacci 3 X.
    """, trace: [:domino])

state.trace.events
|> Enum.reverse()
|> Enum.map(&AL.Trace.payload/1)
|> Enum.map(fn
  entry when is_tuple(entry) -> elem(entry, 0)
  entry -> entry
end)
|> Enum.filter(&(&1 in [:method_call, :method_exit, :clause_call, :clause_exit]))
```

Each of the three recursive `fibonacci` calls opens its own method box immediately wrapping a clause box, and closes them in the same nested order. `method_call, clause_call, ..., clause_exit, method_exit`, one pair of boxes per call, exactly as deep as the recursion goes.

## Turning on tracing for a selector

`AL.trace(selector)` prints every `Call`/`Exit`/`Redo`/`Fail` port as that selector runs, until `AL.notrace()` turns it back off. Since this notebook runs AL in its own process, the output shows up right below the cell. (If you're instead attached to a separate, persistent AL node, the trace prints to that node's own terminal, not the notebook.)

```elixir
AL.trace(:cell)

run(~S"""
  new cell #{name => traced} C.
  """)

AL.notrace()
```

`Fail` shows up the same way when a call doesn't pan out. Here `member` tries `a` against `z`, fails, then `b` against `z`, fails too.

```elixir
AL.trace(:list_member)

run(~S"""
  member [a, b] z.
  """)

AL.notrace()
```

`run` accepts a set of trace flags. With no flags it retains no execution history. `:domino` retains structured method/clause and constraint evidence; `:vm` interleaves every raw goal into the same chronological event stream. The flags compose, so `trace: [:domino, :vm]` gives the former full trace. Every event is tagged as either `:domino` or `:vm`; when both flags are enabled, Domino owns constraint evidence so it is retained only once. A future query explainer can join this same system as another producer.

Dispatch on an open receiver can have several candidate legs- a value/ephemeral leg and a durable leg, at minimum. Tracing shows which legs are being considered before a match is even found.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  @trace_leg_class
  #{super => value}.

  trace_leg_class >> trace_next
  | #{class => trace_leg_class, letter => a} #{class => trace_leg_class, letter => b} |.
  """)

AL.trace(:trace_next)

run(~S"""
  trace_next X #{class => trace_leg_class, letter => b}.
  """)

AL.notrace()
```

## The derivation tree: a cleaner picture than raw trace text

`AL.Trace.derivation_tree/1` turns a completed run state into an operational derivation tree. Call nodes have successful answer children; answers carry the selected clause and what that call derived, and their children are the ordered nested calls and constraint evidence that produced the answer. Open variables nested inside a receiver or argument are included in a call's derivation. Unsuccessful attempts are pruned.
```elixir
{:atomic, {bindings, state}} =
  run(~S"""
    fibonacci 3 X.
    """, trace: [:domino])

[root] = AL.Trace.derivation_tree(state)
[answer] = root.children

{root.kind, root.label, answer.clause, answer.derived, length(answer.children)}
```

When a method has more than one clause that could structurally match, redo tries the next one in place. The tree keeps only the attempt that actually won, not a trail of every clause that was tried.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  @redo_demo
  #{super => object}.

  redo_demo >> pick
  | Self first |.

  redo_demo >> pick
  | Self second |.
  """)
```

```elixir
{:atomic, {bindings, state}} =
  run(~S"""
    new redo_demo #{} Obj.
    pick Obj Result.
    = Result second.
    """, trace: [:domino])

roots = AL.Trace.derivation_tree(state)
pick_node = Enum.find(roots, &match?(%{label: {_, :pick, _}}, &1))

[answer] = pick_node.children
{answer.clause, answer.children, answer.derived}
```

The first clause (`first`) is attempted and then upon fail, redone into the second clause. The derivation tree though, only shows the winner. It prunes the failed branches.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  @probe_box
  #{super => object}.

  probe_box >> probe_reject
  | Self V |
  = V 1,
  > V 50.

  probe_box >> probe_leaf
  | Self 100 |.

  probe_box >> probe_mid
  | Self V |
  probe_leaf Self W,
  = V (+ W 1).

  probe_box >> probe_top
  | Self V |
  probe_mid Self W,
  = V (+ W 1).

  probe_box >> probe_answer
  | Self V |
  probe_reject Self V ; probe_top Self V.
  """)
```

```elixir
{:atomic, {bindings, state}} =
  run(~S"""
    new probe_box #{} Obj.
    probe_answer Obj R.
    """, trace: [:domino])

roots = AL.Trace.derivation_tree(state)
answer = Enum.find(roots, &match?(%{label: {_, :probe_answer, _}}, &1))

[answer_result] = answer.children
[top] = answer_result.children
[top_result] = top.children
[mid] = top_result.children
[mid_result] = mid.children
[leaf] = mid_result.children

{top.label, mid.label, leaf.label, Enum.any?(roots, &match?(%{label: {_, :probe_reject, _}}, &1))}
```

## Constraints show up in the tree too, nested under their own scope

Constraint goals (from [logic and constraints](./logic_and_constraints.livemd)) remain explicit derivation steps inside the successful answer that posted them. Each constraint node carries the variable descriptions immediately before and immediately after that constraint ran. A later method or collection answer cannot be mistaken for something the earlier constraint derived.

```elixir
run(~S"""
  @triple_class
  #{super => object}.

  triple_class >> triple
  | Self N Result |
  = Result (* N 3).
  """)
```

```elixir
{:atomic, {bindings, state}} =
  run(~S"""
    new triple_class #{} Obj.
    triple Obj 4 R.
    """, trace: [:domino])

roots = AL.Trace.derivation_tree(state)

root = Enum.find(roots, &match?(%{label: {_, :triple, _}}, &1))
[answer] = root.children
[constraint_node] = Enum.filter(answer.children, &(&1.kind == :constraint))

{constraint_node.kind, constraint_node.constraints_in, constraint_node.derived}
```
